{"title":"Guest Check-In with Set Membership","lesson":"L1 · Sets and Dictionaries · Set basics","desc":"Use the <code>in</code> operator to check guest names in a set. Then explain why set membership is usually an average <code>O(1)</code> lookup, compared with scanning a list in <code>O(n)</code>.","startFile":"python","goals":[{"id":"creates-registered-set","label":"Creates the registered guest set","target":"python","kind":"regex","rule":"registered\s*=\s*\{","flags":"i","minCount":1},{"id":"uses-in-operator","label":"Uses the in operator for membership checks","target":"python","kind":"regex","rule":"\bin\b","flags":"i","minCount":1},{"id":"prints-results","label":"Prints the check-in results","target":"python","kind":"regex","rule":"print\s*\(","flags":"i","minCount":1},{"id":"explains-constant-lookup","label":"Mentions the average O(1) set lookup","target":"python","kind":"regex","rule":"O\s*\(\s*1\s*\)","flags":"i","minCount":1}],"targetHtml":"<pre><code>Mia: registered
Kai: not registered
Ava: registered
Set membership lookup is average O(1), faster than scanning a list which can take O(n).</code></pre>","targetSub":"Guest check-in results and the set lookup advantage.","files":{"guide":{"html":"","css":"","js":"","python":"registered = {"Ava", "Noah", "Mia", "Leo", "Zoe"}
guests_to_check = ["Mia", "Kai", "Ava"]

for guest in guests_to_check:
    if guest in registered:
        print(f"{guest}: registered")
    else:
        print(f"{guest}: not registered")

lookup_note = (
    "Set membership lookup is average O(1), faster than "
    "scanning a list which can take O(n)."
)
print(lookup_note)"},"pair":{"html":"","css":"","js":"","python":"registered = {"Ava", "Noah", "Mia", "Leo", "Zoe"}
guests_to_check = ["Mia", "Kai", "Ava"]

# 👈 YOUR CODE: complete the membership checks and output for each guest."},"solo":{"html":"","css":"","js":"","python":""}},"solution":{"html":"","css":"","js":"","python":"registered = {"Ava", "Noah", "Mia", "Leo", "Zoe"}
guests_to_check = ["Mia", "Kai", "Ava"]

for guest in guests_to_check:
    if guest in registered:
        print(f"{guest}: registered")
    else:
        print(f"{guest}: not registered")

lookup_note = (
    "Set membership lookup is average O(1), faster than "
    "scanning a list which can take O(n)."
)
print(lookup_note)"},"hints":[{"tier":"nudge","text":"Loop through guests_to_check one guest at a time, then test whether each guest is in registered."},{"tier":"approach","text":"Inside the loop, use an if/else statement: guests found with the in operator are registered; all others are not registered. Finish by printing a note about average O(1) lookup."}],"review":[{"type":"good","text":"You used a set and the in operator to perform clear membership checks."},{"type":"tip","text":"Set membership is usually average O(1), while searching through a list may require checking many items and can take O(n)."}],"guideSteps":[{"explain":"Create a set of registered names and a list of names waiting to be checked.","label":"Prepare the names","code":"registered = {"Ava", "Noah", "Mia", "Leo", "Zoe"}
guests_to_check = ["Mia", "Kai", "Ava"]"},{"explain":"For each guest, use in to choose the correct registered or not registered message.","label":"Check membership","code":"registered = {"Ava", "Noah", "Mia", "Leo", "Zoe"}
guests_to_check = ["Mia", "Kai", "Ava"]

for guest in guests_to_check:
    if guest in registered:
        print(f"{guest}: registered")
    else:
        print(f"{guest}: not registered")"},{"explain":"Print a short explanation of the average O(1) advantage of looking up an item in a set.","label":"Explain the advantage","code":"registered = {"Ava", "Noah", "Mia", "Leo", "Zoe"}
guests_to_check = ["Mia", "Kai", "Ava"]

for guest in guests_to_check:
    if guest in registered:
        print(f"{guest}: registered")
    else:
        print(f"{guest}: not registered")

lookup_note = (
    "Set membership lookup is average O(1), faster than "
    "scanning a list which can take O(n)."
)
print(lookup_note)"}],"blooms_level":"Applying","difficulty_level":"medium","difficulty_rating":5,"questionType":"code_challenge","visualMatchThreshold":90,"metadata":{"subject":"Python","courseLevel":"Level 1","module":"Sets and Dictionaries","topic":"Sets and Dictionaries","topicType":"Concept","topicFamily":"Sets and Dictionaries","subtopic":"Set basics","learning_objective":"Use the in operator on a set and reason about its O(1) lookup advantage.","bloomsVerb":"Use","cognitiveTier":"Lower-Order","skillArea":"mixed"}}

{"title":"Build a Book Dictionary","lesson":"L1 · Sets and Dictionaries · Dictionary basics","desc":"A dictionary is an unordered collection of key-value pairs. Create a <code>book</code> dictionary with <code>title</code>, <code>author</code>, and <code>year</code> keys, then print the stored values.","startFile":"python","goals":[{"id":"has-dictionary","label":"Creates a book dictionary","target":"python","kind":"regex","rule":"book\s*=\s*\{","flags":"i","minCount":1},{"id":"has-author-key","label":"Adds an author key","target":"python","kind":"regex","rule":"author\s*:","flags":"i","minCount":1},{"id":"has-year-key","label":"Adds a year key","target":"python","kind":"regex","rule":"year\s*:","flags":"i","minCount":1},{"id":"uses-print","label":"Prints dictionary values","target":"python","kind":"regex","rule":"print\s*\(","flags":"i","minCount":1}],"targetHtml":"<pre><code>Title: The Little Prince
