{"title":"Escape the Global Trap","lesson":"Python · Local and global variables · global and nonlocal","desc":"A checkout program uses a global <code>discount_rate</code> inside <code>calculate_total()</code>. This hidden dependency makes tests difficult: a test must change shared state, and one test can affect another. Critique the design by rewriting the function so the discount rate is an explicit parameter, then add independent assertions for two different discounts and print the results. Do not use <code>global</code> to solve the problem.","startFile":"python","goals":[{"id":"parameterizes-discount","label":"Makes the discount rate an explicit parameter","target":"python","kind":"regex","rule":"def\s+calculate_total\s*\([^)]*discount_rate","flags":"i","minCount":1},{"id":"returns-calculated-total","label":"Returns a total using the supplied discount","target":"python","kind":"regex","rule":"return\s+price\s*\*\s*\(\s*1\s*-\s*discount_rate","flags":"i","minCount":1},{"id":"uses-independent-rates","label":"Calls the function with multiple explicit rates","target":"python","kind":"regex","rule":"calculate_total\s*\(\s*100\s*,\s*0\.\d+\s*\)","flags":"i","minCount":2},{"id":"adds-tests","label":"Adds assertions that test the function","target":"python","kind":"regex","rule":"assert\s+calculate_total\s*\(","flags":"i","minCount":2}],"targetHtml":"<pre><code>10% discount: 90.0
25% discount: 75.0</code></pre>","targetSub":"Two independent discount calculations are tested and printed.","files":{"guide":{"html":"","css":"","js":"","python":"# The rate is now an input rather than a global.
def calculate_total(price, discount_rate):
    return price * (1 - discount_rate)

assert calculate_total(100, 0.10) == 90.0
assert calculate_total(100, 0.25) == 75.0

print("10% discount:", calculate_total(100, 0.10))
print("25% discount:", calculate_total(100, 0.25))"},"pair":{"html":"","css":"","js":"","python":"# This version relies on hidden global state.
discount_rate = 0.10

def calculate_total(price):
    return price * (1 - discount_rate)

# 👈 YOUR CODE: Refactor calculate_total so discount_rate is a parameter.
# Add two assert tests using 0.10 and 0.25, then print both results."},"solo":{"html":"","css":"","js":"","python":""}},"solution":{"html":"","css":"","js":"","python":"# Critique: the old version read discount_rate from global state.
# A parameter makes every call and test independent.
def calculate_total(price, discount_rate):
    return price * (1 - discount_rate)

assert calculate_total(100, 0.10) == 90.0
assert calculate_total(100, 0.25) == 75.0

print("10% discount:", calculate_total(100, 0.10))
print("25% discount:", calculate_total(100, 0.25))"},"hints":[{"tier":"nudge","text":"Instead of reading discount_rate from outside the function, list it between the function's parentheses."},{"tier":"approach","text":"Define calculate_total(price, discount_rate), return the calculated value, and call it separately with 0.10 and 0.25. Assertions can confirm both expected totals."}],"review":[{"type":"good","text":"You replaced a hidden global dependency with an explicit parameter and tested the function with independent inputs."},{"type":"tip","text":"Try adding edge-case tests, such as a 0% discount or a 100% discount, without changing any shared variable."}],"guideSteps":[{"explain":"This original function depends on a module-level value, so changing the discount for one test changes shared state.","label":"Spot the hidden dependency","code":"# The original design hides this setting in global state.
discount_rate = 0.10

def calculate_total(price):
    return price * (1 - discount_rate)

print("Current total:", calculate_total(100))"},{"explain":"Pass discount_rate into the function so each call states exactly which value it uses.","label":"Make the dependency explicit","code":"# The rate is now an input rather than a global.
def calculate_total(price, discount_rate):
    return price * (1 - discount_rate)

assert calculate_total(100, 0.10) == 90.0
print("10% discount:", calculate_total(100, 0.10))"},{"explain":"Use a second assertion and call to show that tests can use different rates without modifying shared state.","label":"Test independent calls","code":"# The rate is now an input rather than a global.
def calculate_total(price, discount_rate):
    return price * (1 - discount_rate)

assert calculate_total(100, 0.10) == 90.0
assert calculate_total(100, 0.25) == 75.0

print("10% discount:", calculate_total(100, 0.10))
print("25% discount:", calculate_total(100, 0.25))"}],"blooms_level":"Evaluating","difficulty_level":"hard","difficulty_rating":4,"questionType":"code_challenge","visualMatchThreshold":90,"metadata":{"subject":"Python","courseLevel":"Level 1","module":"Local and global variables","topic":"Local and global variables","topicType":"Concept","topicFamily":"Local and global variables","subtopic":"global and nonlocal","learning_objective":"Critique an example where reliance on globals makes a program hard to test.","bloomsVerb":"Critique","cognitiveTier":"Higher-Order","skillArea":"mixed"}}

