rectangle = Rectangle(6, 4)
print("Area:", rectangle.area)"},"hints":[{"tier":"nudge","text":"Place @property directly above a method named area."},{"tier":"approach","text":"Inside area, return self._width * self._height. Then access it as rectangle.area without parentheses."}],"review":[{"type":"good","text":"You used @property to expose area as a computed attribute instead of calling a method."},{"type":"tip","text":"Properties can also validate values or provide formatted information while keeping the internal attributes protected."}],"guideSteps":[{"explain":"Define a Rectangle class and store its dimensions in underscore-prefixed attributes.","label":"Create the class","code":"class Rectangle:
    def __init__(self, width, height):
        self._width = width
        self._height = height"},{"explain":"Use @property so area can calculate and expose the rectangle's area like an ordinary attribute.","label":"Add the property","code":"class Rectangle:
